# When you pay for computation

Functional against imperative, program against type, inferred against written signatures: each looks like a border between two kinds of thing. Each is a decision about when a computation runs and who remembers its result.

::exhibit{id="two-machines" place="wide"}

## The two models compute the same functions

A Turing machine is a head over a tape. Each step reads a cell, writes a cell, moves and changes state. The tape remembers everything, so a program never says what it carries forward.

::exhibit{id="turing" place="wide"}

The lambda calculus has no tape. A program is an expression, and running it means substituting arguments into function bodies. Nothing is overwritten. Anything a later step needs has to be handed to it.

The term below is a program in that picture. A number is a function that applies its first argument that many times, so `succ 2` is a function applied to a function. A step contracts a redex, an application of a [lambda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambda_calculus#Reduction) to an argument, and the order is a choice the machine usually hides. Normal order reaches a normal form whenever one exists, and applicative order can loop forever on a term that has one. Run `ignore omega` in both orders.

::exhibit{id="lambda" place="wide"}

Turing proved in 1936 that the two define the same functions, and Church and Kleene had tied lambda terms to the recursive functions the same year<Cite id="sep-ct" />. Imperative languages grew from the first picture and functional ones from the second, and neither computes anything the other cannot. What differs is bookkeeping: an imperative program lets the machine remember state; a functional program writes the state down as an argument.

## do-notation converts one into the other

Lean's `do` writes the tape picture inside the lambda picture. A counter in `StateM`:

```lean
def tick : StateM Nat Unit := modify (· + 1)

def threeTicks : StateM Nat Nat := do
  tick
  tick
  tick
  get

#eval threeTicks.run' 0   -- 3
```

The reference gives the rewrite: `do e1; es` becomes `e1 >>= fun () => do es`, and `let x ← e1; es` becomes `e1 >>= fun x => do es`<Cite id="lean-do" />. The Haskell report specifies the same translation with `>>` and `>>=`<Cite id="haskell-do" />. `StateM Nat Nat` unfolds to `Nat → Nat × Nat`, so the mutable counter is this function:

```lean
def threeTicksByHand (s : Nat) : Nat × Nat :=
  let s := s + 1
  let s := s + 1
  let s := s + 1
  (s, s)

example : threeTicks = threeTicksByHand := rfl
```

Lean accepts the `rfl`: the two definitions are equal by unfolding alone. The tape came back as an argument. Moggi gave effects such as state a semantics as monads over a pure calculus<Cite id="moggi" />, and Wadler turned that into a way to write programs<Cite id="wadler" />.

Compilers run the conversion the other way. The STG machine, the abstract machine GHC compiles Haskell to, keeps unevaluated expressions as closures in a heap and forces one by pushing a continuation on a stack<Cite id="stg" />.

## The type checker is a program too

Computation can also move earlier, into the compiler. Rust's trait solver runs this addition while it type checks:

```rust
use std::marker::PhantomData;
struct Z;
struct S<N>(PhantomData<N>);

trait Add<B> { type Out; }
impl<B> Add<B> for Z { type Out = B; }
impl<A: Add<B>, B> Add<B> for S<A> { type Out = S<A::Out>; }

trait Val { const N: u32; }
impl Val for Z { const N: u32 = 0; }
impl<N: Val> Val for S<N> { const N: u32 = N::N + 1; }

fn main() {
    // 2 + 3, worked out by the trait solver while type checking
    println!("{}", <<S<S<Z>> as Add<S<S<S<Z>>>>>::Out as Val>::N); // 5
}
```

The traits are a full language. Shea Leffler built an interpreter for Smallfuck, a known Turing-complete language, out of them<Cite id="leffler" />. Veldhuizen sketched the same result for C++ templates<Cite id="veldhuizen" />, and [TypeScript issue #14833](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/14833) shows it for TypeScript's types. Nix has its own version of the move: with [IFD](/thoughts/ifd), evaluation stops to run a build so a later step can read its output.

That is computation over types, and it is blind to run time. The solver can add `S<S<Z>>` to `S<S<S<Z>>>`; it can never see a number the user types. A dependent type can, because in Lean the language of types and the language of values are the same language. A value chooses a type:

```lean
def Tuple : Nat → Type
  | 0 => Unit
  | n + 1 => Nat × Tuple n

def mkTuple : (n : Nat) → Tuple n
  | 0 => ()
  | n + 1 => (n, mkTuple n)

#check mkTuple 3   -- mkTuple 3 : Tuple 3
#eval mkTuple 3    -- (2, 1, 0, ())
```

`Tuple 3` is `Nat × Nat × Nat × Unit`, and `rfl` proves it. Delete the signature of `mkTuple` and Lean 4.30 rejects it:

```
error: Type mismatch
  (n, mkTuple n)
has type
  Nat × Unit
but is expected to have type
  Unit
```

The elaborator typed the first arm, fixed `Unit` for every `n`, and the second arm contradicted it. Succeeding would mean inventing `Tuple` from the arms: a function from values to types. Dowek proved that typability in the λΠ-calculus, the core of dependent types, is undecidable, so "some type information has to be mentioned by the programmer"<Cite id="dowek" />. `(n : Nat) → Tuple n` is the line inference cannot write.

## Inference is paid on every read

Where inference works, someone still pays. `def main := IO.println "hi"` compiles, and `#check main` prints `main : IO Unit`. That type lives in the editor's hover and the compiled module, never in the text a reviewer reads, so every reader derives it again. A written signature is that derivation stored once.

It also stores intent. Inference reports what the body happens to do. Edit the body of a public definition and its inferred type moves with it; the error surfaces at a caller in another file. With the type written, the same edit fails where it was made. So write signatures on public definitions and skip them on local `let`s and lambdas, where every caller is in view.

## Text files are not the unit of code

Both reasons assume code is text that gets re-read. Unison stores something else. A definition "is identified by a hash of its syntax tree", and names "are just separately stored metadata that don't affect the function's hash"<Cite id="unison-idea" />. The codebase is a database ([a SQLite file](https://github.com/unisonweb/unison/blob/a1652e3ada28924cb9e75ff1d62408ee8df6b78f/parser-typechecker/src/Unison/Codebase/SqliteCodebase/Paths.hs) at `.unison/v2/unison.sqlite3`) of definitions already typechecked: "Once anyone has parsed and typechecked a definition and added it to the codebase, no one has to do that ever again."<Cite id="unison-idea" />

Here is a toy of that codebase. A hash is four digits of FNV-1a over a definition's body and the hashes of what it uses, so they are mine and not Unison's. Editing a definition gives every definition that uses it a new hash and leaves the rest untouched. Edit `config` and `parse` stays reused. Edit `util` and all four definitions built on it change. Switch to rename and click a name: no hash moves.

::exhibit{id="merkle" place="wide"}

Once the hash is the identity, whole classes of recomputation stop:

- A rename edits metadata, so nothing downstream recompiles. Two libraries pinned to different versions of a function hold two hashes, and "the diamond dependency problem [is] just not a thing"<Cite id="unison-idea" />.
- A pure test is a function of its dependencies' hashes, so its result is a cache entry: "There's no need to rerun a deterministic test if none of its dependencies have changed!"<Cite id="unison-idea" />
- Computation moves to the data. "The sender ships the bytecode tree to the recipient, who inspects the bytecode for any hashes it's missing"<Cite id="unison-idea" />, asks for those and runs. Only missing hashes cross the wire.

The inferred type is another stored fact you query, so the amortization argument for signatures disappears. The promise argument changes shape. An edit makes a new hash; `update` retypechecks the dependents, keeps the ones that pass and opens the rest in your editor<Cite id="unison-update" />. The break still lands at the edit, because the codebase knows every caller.

Effects get the same treatment. Abilities are do-notation without a monad: the type carries the effect, `Nat -> Nat ->{Exception} Nat`, the body is written in direct style, and the docs say direct-style effects and monads "have the same expressive power"<Cite id="unison-abilities" />. The code reads like the tape; the type keeps the lambda calculus's books.

## Where the picture breaks

**Church-Turing is silent about cost.** It is a thesis about what is computable, unprovable because "effectively calculable" is informal<Cite id="sep-ct" />. Whether a beta step is a fair unit of time stayed open because a linear number of steps can build an exponentially large term. Accattoli and Dal Lago settled it in 2016: leftmost-outermost steps are a reasonable cost model once results stay shared<Cite id="accattoli" />.

**rustc is a bounded machine.** An unbounded trait requirement fails with E0275 and a hint to raise `#![recursion_limit]`; the default is 128<Cite id="rust-limits" />. This confirms the rule. A Turing machine has an unbounded tape and rustc has a depth of 128; what Turing completeness buys in practice is that the bound is a setting you can raise.

**Recursion alone does not force a signature.** `def countdown n := if n = 0 then 0 else countdown (n - 1)` is accepted and `#check` prints `countdown (n : Nat) : Nat`. The signature becomes mandatory when the type depends on a value.

**Hashes decide sameness of syntax.** Renames are free and identical definitions are shared, but `x + 1` and `1 + x` hash differently. Whether two programs behave the same is undecidable in general ([Rice's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice%27s_theorem)), so a hash can only record that two trees match.

## Write down what cannot be derived

A type is a computation scheduled before the program runs. A signature is one you scheduled by hand. Write it where no machine can schedule it for you: a type that depends on a value, or a promise to callers you have not met.

<References />
